<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mykola Skrypnyk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1872">1872</a>–<a href="/years/1933">1933</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mykola Skrypnyk was a Ukrainian Bolshevik who served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peoples-secretariat">People's Secretariat</a> of the Soviet Ukrainian government formed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kharkiv">Kharkiv</a> in December 1917. Later, as People's Commissar of Education of Soviet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukraine">Ukraine</a>, he promoted Ukrainian language and culture under the policy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ukrainisation">Ukrainisation</a>. Attacked as a nationalist when Stalin reversed that policy, he took his own life in 1933.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mykola-skrypnyk.jpg" alt="Mykola Skrypnyk" width="320" height="486" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: unknown-anonymous · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skrypnyk_Mykola.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
